ce  take a look at this sentence here this  means I want to eat something  in Indonesian it's samo mackenson  swatter in malay it's sayin at McCann  says what to the verb meaning to want is  different in Indonesian Mao is probably  the most common word in Malay Honda is  more common and in casual speech it's  often abbreviated to na Honda is also  used in Indonesian but less often and mo  is also used in Malay but less  frequently another sentence this means I  always wake up after 9 o'clock in  Indonesian saseru balanced laughs Jamila  in Malay scientist evangeline syllabus  gem symbol a word for word both  sentences are I always wake up after our  9 in Malay send si is often used for  always those Salah Lou is also common in  casual speech in Indonesian Salah Lu is  the most common word Cynthia is rare but  it's longer form sin auntie aza is used  in more formal situations and the most  common word for after in this context in  Indonesian is satella and in Malay  syllabus some words here and there are  spelled a little differently one of the  words meaning to want in Indonesian is  Mao as we saw before in Malay it's mahu  Mao the word for different in Indonesian  is big  while in Malay it's busy the word for  try in Indonesian is soba while in Malay  it's Juba the word for healthy is sad in  Indonesian while it's tsehai in Malay so  far I've mainly been focusing on  standard Indonesian and standard Malay  which are in large part the same except  for some differences in vocabulary and  vocabulary usage but in casual speech  the differences can be much greater  there are numerous regional and local  dialects of Malay and even though  Indonesian has learned as a second  language by most Indonesians it has  developed various local varieties  influenced by regional languages of  Indonesia me and her went to the shop to  buy some food then we ate together first  let's compare the standard sentences in  standard Indonesian oh god Andy Aparna  Kotoko in Doha me I'm a fan 